WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.470
- Hi, my name is Alan
Gerber and I'm the director

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.470
of the Institution for
Social and Policy Studies,

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:15.453
the sponsor of tonight's event.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.710
Tonight we're gonna be
having a panel discussion,

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:22.240
excuse me, a few speakers
followed by a question

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:23.660
and answer period.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:25.420
The topic for tonight's session

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.220
is what did the voters do,
and why did they do it?

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.570
And so for this event,
we've gathered four experts

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:37.090
on political behavior,
American political behavior,

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:40.830
to put what happened on election day

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:45.220
and after election day into perspective

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:48.800
and shed some light on
the science of the study

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.883
and understanding of voter behavior.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:54.790
Tonight we're gonna have a
question and answer period

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:56.560
and here are some of the logistics

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.890
associated with that and
the rest of the event.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:03.980
Please send questions to the panelists

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.370
using the question and answer button

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:08.523
at the bottom of the Zoom screen.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.430
The questions ideally
should be relatively short,

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:16.990
so please try not to write a paragraph

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.870
if you think you can
write maybe a sentence

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:21.460
or a sentence and a half.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:24.920
It'll be a lot easier for us
to process those questions.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.320
The event is being recorded.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.877
If you need technical assistance
please email isps@yale.edu,

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:38.400
so that's isps@yale.edu.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.013
And here's the format for tonight's event.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.610
Following my introductory,
welcoming remarks

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:50.300
and biographical details
about tonight's panelists,

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:53.920
each of the panelists will
talk for about seven minutes,

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.240
sharing a few of their critical
insights, their key insights

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:00.093
about the election with you.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.100
And then we're gonna have a
question and answer period.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:06.010
So you can start sending
your questions along

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:08.530
whenever you have them.

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:10.140
And they'll be gathered up

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:14.140
and I'll ask them of the panelists

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.313
after their remarks have concluded.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.980
That part of the session
will go about 30 minutes,

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:24.490
I would imagine.

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:28.530
Great, so tonight let me now
begin welcoming the panelists

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:29.790
in order of their appearance.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:33.720
They are going to be speaking
in alphabetical order.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.053
We're gonna begin with Alex Coppock.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:41.130
Alex is an assistant professor
of political science at Yale,

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:42.790
and a resident faculty fellow

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:45.840
at the Institution of
Social and Policy Studies

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.060
and Center For The Study
of American Politics.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:51.000
And Alex's research lies

00:02:52.527 --> 00:02:55.270
in the study of political persuasion

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:58.100
and its implications for the malleability

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:01.110
of public opinion in the
context of elections.

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:02.760
He's an expert on the design

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.920
and analysis of randomized experiments

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:08.710
and he won the Best Software
Award for DeclareDesign

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:10.780
and that was awarded by the

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:12.770
American Political Science Association

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:15.920
Political Methodology Section in 2019.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:20.900
Alex is working on two books now,

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:24.560
one on research design
and one on persuasion.

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:25.450
If you're interested,

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:28.263
you can check out some
of Alex's scholarship.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.210
One of his recent papers
in science advances

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:36.210
is on the subject he'll be
talking about in his remarks

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:38.860
that's a paper called "The small effects

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:43.310
of political advertising are
small regardless of context,

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:45.950
message, sender, or receiver,

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:49.640
evidence from 59 real time
randomized experiments."

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:53.513
So you can check that out
and other of Alex's works.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:57.130
Next, we'll be hearing from Josh Kalla.

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:58.970
Josh is also an assistant professor

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:01.440
of political science at Yale.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.100
He's also a professor in
the Department of Statistics

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:05.630
and Data Science.

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:07.690
He's a resident fellow at the Institution

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:09.740
for Social and Policy Studies.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:13.710
Josh studies political
persuasion, prejudice reduction,

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:16.940
and decision making among
voters and political elites,

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:19.513
primarily through the
use of field experiments.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:23.020
Kalla was awarded the
Leamer-Rosenthal Prize

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:25.240
for Open Social Science,

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:29.160
and his work has also been
awarded the Cialdini prize

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:32.800
by the Society for Personality
and Social Psychology,

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.010
an award that recognizes the publication

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:38.520
that best explicates social
psychological phenomena,

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:41.493
principally through the use
of field research methods.

00:04:42.831 --> 00:04:45.320
Josh's scholarship has been published

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:48.390
in numerous academic journals.

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:51.277
If you're interested in
reading more check out

00:04:51.277 --> 00:04:53.420
"Reducing exclusionary attitudes

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:56.080
through interpersonal conversation,"

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:58.070
published in a recent issue

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:00.323
of the American Political Science Review.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:03.833
Welcome, Josh.

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:09.193
Next, we'll hear from Mike Podhorzer.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:12.610
Mike is assistant to the president

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:16.380
for strategic research at the AFL-CIO.

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.380
He's a leading political
strategist and analyst

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:24.020
for over 30 decades and has
led the federation strategy

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:27.640
to create permanent, year-round
mobilization capacity

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:31.410
to elect worker-friendly
candidates, advocates for issues,

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:34.760
and demand greater accountability
from elected officials

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:38.150
independent from political
party structures.

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:39.900
He established Working America,

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:43.170
a community partner
organization that has grown

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:46.130
to three million non-union
members nationwide,

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:48.910
reflecting a focus on
all working families,

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:51.963
whether or not they are covered
by collective bargaining.

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:55.340
Podhorzer led the federation's work

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:59.210
on voter protection efforts
and voter mobilization strategy

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:01.440
during the most recent election.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:04.790
He is a recognized leader in his field

00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:08.780
and he was also a pioneer
in data-driven politics

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:14.870
and is admired by all
those who are interested

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:18.523
in the science of voter behavior.

00:06:19.671 --> 00:06:22.021
And then we'll be hearing from.

00:06:22.021 --> 00:06:25.300
Oh I should also say, he was
one of the founding chairs

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:29.380
of the Analyst's Institute, one
of the leading organizations

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:33.850
for bringing science to campaign activity.

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:36.433
And then we'll be hearing
from Lynn Vavreck.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:41.810
Lynn is the Marvin Hoffenberg
professor of American politics

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:44.590
and public policy at UCLA.

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:48.940
She was PI of the 2020
election project Nationscape,

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:51.290
the largest study of
presidential elections

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:53.820
ever fielded in the United States.

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:55.290
The project has been interviewing

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:57.870
more than 6,000 people a week

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:01.170
and will complete 500,000 interviews

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:05.080
before the inauguration in 2021.

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:06.430
Are you still in the field?

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:10.600
Great, that's really good to hear,

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.900
'cause this was one of those elections

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:16.310
where you were wondering if it would end.

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:18.130
And so it's great that
you're still in the field.

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:21.913
Did you decide that before the election?

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:24.150
- Through inauguration day.

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:26.513
- That's pressing, pression, very good.

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:32.230
Lynn was a recipient of
the Andrew Carnegie prize,

00:07:32.230 --> 00:07:34.680
one of the kind of big fellowship awards

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:36.610
in the social sciences.

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:39.400
And I don't know if it
helped fund the research

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:42.230
that I just described or
not, but it probably funded

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:45.760
some important studies
that you've conducted.

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:48.587
Lynn is the author of
five books including,

00:07:48.587 --> 00:07:52.800
"Identity Crisis: The 2016
Presidential Campaign,"

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:55.310
and the "Battle for the
Meaning of America,"

00:07:55.310 --> 00:08:00.263
described as the most ominous
book on the 2016 election.

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:04.180
Lynn is also a contributing
columnist to The Upshot

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:05.990
at the New York Times.

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:08.570
Lynn is a leading scholar
of American politics

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:11.110
and American elections and it's wonderful

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:14.093
that she's here joining us
from Los Angeles tonight.

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:19.460
With that, why don't we just
turn it over to our panelists.

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:23.800
They will speak without
interruption in between their talks.

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:27.370
Alex, Josh, Mike, and Lynn in that order.

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:30.550
And then we'll open it up
for question and answer.

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:31.593
Thank you very much.

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:37.490
- Thank you Alan.

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:40.010
Thanks everyone for joining tonight.

00:08:40.010 --> 00:08:41.827
The paper that Alan referenced is

00:08:41.827 --> 00:08:45.120
"The small effects of political
advertising are small."

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:47.720
And it has two smalls
in the title which was,

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:49.097
like the editor of the journal was like,

00:08:49.097 --> 00:08:50.560
"Are you sure there's not a typo here?"

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:51.393
I'm like, "No, no, no.

00:08:51.393 --> 00:08:54.370
The small effects of political
advertising are indeed small

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:56.050
regardless of all of
the things in the title,

00:08:56.050 --> 00:08:58.390
so context, and the
kind of person you are,

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:00.020
and the time of the elections.

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:02.190
That's what that paper finds.

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:03.560
And I'd love to talk more about that,

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:08.210
but I actually prepared a
different topic for you.

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:12.780
It's about the pre-election polls

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:15.170
and the difference between
the pre-election polls

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:17.080
and what actually occurred.

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:19.920
And so the source of biases in the polls

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.780
are something that we need
to shake out as a community.

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:24.100
We need to figure out why it is

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:29.100
we're unable to seem to
estimate with as much accuracy

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:33.240
as we would like what the
vote margins are gonna be.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:35.670
And I wanna talk about one source of bias

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:37.670
that I actually don't think is a problem.

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:40.810
And it comes up a lot, which is this idea

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:43.733
that some people, when we
get them in the interview,

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:46.350
we ask them who they're gonna vote for

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:48.730
and they are embarrassed,
or they're worried

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:51.170
that they will experience
shame if they admit

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:52.100
who they're gonna vote for.

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:54.510
And this happened in
particular with respect,

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:56.910
or the idea is that it
happened in particular

00:09:56.910 --> 00:09:58.820
with respect to Trump voters.

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:02.027
So because Donald Trump
is associated with racist

00:10:02.027 --> 00:10:05.950
and sexist views, people
who are Trump supporters

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:08.950
were worried that if
they admitted in a survey

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:10.080
that they are Trump supporters,

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:14.040
that they would themselves
be seen as racist or sexist.

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:17.530
So that's an example of sensitivity bias.

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:22.530
So if a question is sensitive
it's because I think

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:26.300
that if I say my true answer
that I will experience

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:29.730
some cost, perhaps in
embarrassment or other things.

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:32.280
And sensitivity bias is a real thing.

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:37.210
So, in authoritarian
regimes we can demonstrate

00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:41.280
that people over report support
for authoritarian regimes

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:45.350
because, perhaps rightly,
they expect bad consequences

00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:48.480
if it is found out that they
don't support the regime.

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:51.050
So we know that through these techniques

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:55.980
that indirectly estimate
support for the regime,

00:10:55.980 --> 00:10:59.300
or in this case, indirectly estimate

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:02.150
support for Donald Trump.

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:04.210
So in the 2016 cycle, I did a thing

00:11:04.210 --> 00:11:05.220
called a list experiment.

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:08.010
And this list experiment
is an indirect method

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:11.120
of estimating support for a candidate

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:13.420
that doesn't require a person

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:17.460
to directly admit their true beliefs,

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:19.160
but they can let us know in another way.

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:21.070
And I'm gonna share my
screen to show you an example

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:23.203
of what that experiment looked like.

00:11:25.090 --> 00:11:29.930
So what we did is we split a
sample of survey respondents

00:11:29.930 --> 00:11:32.880
into two groups, a control
group and a treatment group.

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:35.677
And the control group was
asked the question on the left.

00:11:35.677 --> 00:11:38.380
"So please tell me how many
of these following things

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:39.270
you would do.

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:42.250
We don't want to know
which of them you would do,

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:43.440
just how many."

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:44.397
So the list is:

00:11:44.397 --> 00:11:46.070
"If it were up for a vote, I would vote

00:11:46.070 --> 00:11:47.240
to raise the minimum wage.

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:48.940
If it up for a vote,
I would vote to repeal

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:50.460
the Affordable Care Act.

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:53.280
If it were up for a vote, I
would vote ban assault weapons."

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:55.320
So we don't say which of
those things you would do,

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:56.220
you just say how many.

00:11:56.220 --> 00:11:58.430
So perhaps your answer is two.

00:11:58.430 --> 00:12:00.600
If you were assigned
to the treatment group,

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:03.447
you saw those same three
things, plus a fourth item.

00:12:03.447 --> 00:12:06.510
"If the 2016 presidential
election were being held today

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:08.190
and the candidates were Clinton and Trump,

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:09.550
I would vote for Trump."

00:12:09.550 --> 00:12:12.300
And so if you then say, two.

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:14.720
I don't know whether you
would vote for Donald Trump

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:15.990
or you would do one of these other things.

00:12:15.990 --> 00:12:17.610
So this is a way in which we would protect

00:12:17.610 --> 00:12:20.070
survey respondent's identity.

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:21.780
If we then take the average difference

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:23.130
between the treatment and control group

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:25.720
that forms an estimate of the fraction

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:27.070
who support Donald Trump.

00:12:27.070 --> 00:12:29.700
Okay, so cat's outta
the bag, when I did this

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:32.060
I found no difference, none,

00:12:32.060 --> 00:12:34.880
between the list experiment estimate

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:36.560
and the direct question estimate.

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:39.160
So this indirect thing,
which made it so that people

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:40.500
didn't have to directly admit

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:42.970
and be embarrassed about their
support for Donald Trump,

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:44.880
it didn't change our estimate

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:46.550
of the support for Donald Trump.

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:49.800
So, if we look on the left
we have strong Democrats,

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:52.660
the small dot is the
direct-question estimate,

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:55.260
the big whiskers, that's the effect

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:57.170
from the list experiment.

00:12:57.170 --> 00:13:00.560
And we can see that since the
confidence levels overlapped,

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:02.690
it's approximately the same answer.

00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:06.810
And that pattern persists
for all seven flavors

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:08.810
of party identification that we measured

00:13:12.260 --> 00:13:13.590
in this experiment.

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:17.840
So, establish in the
2016, that I don't think

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:20.610
that shy Trump supporters,
the sensitivity bias

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:23.020
with respect to the
presidential vote twist question

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:25.020
is a major source of bias.

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:26.107
I didn't do it again in 2020,

00:13:26.107 --> 00:13:29.050
but there were other clues
that sensitivity bias

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:33.870
is not a major group factor in
explaining the polling miss.

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:36.180
So, on the left-hand side, so this facet,

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:38.280
the presidential facet, this
is not a graphic that I made,

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:40.570
this is made by Thomas Wood,

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:43.450
who's just an unbelievable
data visualizer.

00:13:43.450 --> 00:13:45.730
I'm in awe of what he's
able to do so quickly.

00:13:45.730 --> 00:13:48.263
He was making graphs like
this on election night.

00:13:49.730 --> 00:13:54.730
In each state we're seeing
the pre-election polls

00:13:55.190 --> 00:13:58.900
minus the actual vote
totals in each state.

00:13:58.900 --> 00:14:03.310
And we see that over here,
we are getting really bad,

00:14:03.310 --> 00:14:06.463
we're missing by 15 points
in Biden's direction.

00:14:07.532 --> 00:14:08.890
What we can see for sensitivity bias

00:14:08.890 --> 00:14:11.400
is that we missed the
same states for Senate

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:14.230
that we miss for the presidential.

00:14:14.230 --> 00:14:16.830
So, if people are uncomfortable,

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:19.270
you'd have to believe that
they're also uncomfortable

00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:21.640
admitting their support for Susan Collins,

00:14:21.640 --> 00:14:26.230
as they are uncomfortable
admitting their support for Trump.

00:14:26.230 --> 00:14:28.260
And we don't think that that's the case.

00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:29.470
So what we think instead

00:14:29.470 --> 00:14:32.460
it's not once we get them in the survey

00:14:32.460 --> 00:14:33.820
are they lying to us,

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:35.600
the problem is before then.

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:39.240
The problem is we're not
reaching the right people.

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:42.090
And so, with response rates what they are

00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:44.690
we're having to do all
the statistical wizardry

00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:47.610
to try and re-weight the
polls in order to get

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:48.690
a more accurate picture

00:14:48.690 --> 00:14:50.640
of what's gonna happen on election day.

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:53.700
And for two election cycles in a row,

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:56.910
our re-weighting strategy
has not been successful.

00:14:56.910 --> 00:14:58.750
Now, I'm in the privileged position

00:14:58.750 --> 00:15:01.090
of not having to actually
do that hard work myself,

00:15:01.090 --> 00:15:02.320
so I'm throwing stones.

00:15:02.320 --> 00:15:04.770
But I don't think it's
because of sensitivity bias,

00:15:04.770 --> 00:15:08.480
I think that it's due to
selection into the sample.

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:09.880
So that's all I have for you.

00:15:09.880 --> 00:15:11.513
I think Josh, you're up next.

00:15:13.970 --> 00:15:17.220
- Thank you so much Alex
for opening that up.

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:19.200
And thank you all for joining us,

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:21.990
taking time out of your
evening to come listen to us

00:15:21.990 --> 00:15:24.120
talk about the world of politics.

00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:29.120
So, today I wanna talk
about what I think matters

00:15:29.950 --> 00:15:33.680
in presidential campaigns and
how this sometimes differs

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:36.550
from what some analysts,
what some talking heads say

00:15:36.550 --> 00:15:39.730
and support what other
analysts and talking heads say.

00:15:39.730 --> 00:15:42.120
I wanna kinda stake a
claim in this argument

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:44.730
as to what matters in
presidential campaigns.

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:47.260
And to illustrate the next step in this,

00:15:47.260 --> 00:15:49.560
I just wanna use the example of Texas.

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:51.930
Everything that I'm going
to say you could apply

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:54.100
in different states, you could
apply to different candidates

00:15:54.100 --> 00:15:55.370
and presidential races,

00:15:55.370 --> 00:15:59.320
but Texas is a nice example
coming outta the 2020 election.

00:15:59.320 --> 00:16:01.940
And one of the big questions
I have been asked is

00:16:01.940 --> 00:16:05.200
why did Joe Biden lose ground
with Latinos in south Texas

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:08.700
and more broadly, why
did Joe Biden lose Texas?

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:11.220
And coming outta the race you see

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:13.550
two very competing explanations,

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:16.210
and the TexasMonthly has done a nice job

00:16:16.210 --> 00:16:17.320
interviewing folks on the ground

00:16:17.320 --> 00:16:20.160
and trying to get their
interpretation of the race.

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:23.990
And reading kinda the popular
press version of the election

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:26.560
you see two competing arguments.

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:30.370
One is exemplified nicely by Chuck Rocha,

00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:32.220
who used to work for Senator Sanders

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:34.230
and now runs an organization tryin'

00:16:34.230 --> 00:16:36.810
to organize Latino voters.

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:39.590
And what Chuck says is

00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:41.530
it's really a matter of campaign tactics.

00:16:41.530 --> 00:16:44.000
He's quoted as saying that
the reason why Joe Biden

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:45.257
lost in Texas is, quote,

00:16:45.257 --> 00:16:47.180
"nobody was knockin' on people's doors

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:50.090
and letting them know why
they should vote for Biden."

00:16:50.090 --> 00:16:52.220
And that's kind of one very
common argument you see

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:54.350
throughout the presidential election,

00:16:54.350 --> 00:16:55.730
it's all about tactics.

00:16:55.730 --> 00:16:59.040
If Biden had done more of X,
or if Trump had done more of X,

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:01.920
they would have won
rather than someone else.

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:03.700
On the other side you see an argument

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:05.290
where it's not about tactics,

00:17:05.290 --> 00:17:07.410
it's very much one about messaging.

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:10.590
And this I think is explained
nicely by Joacim Hernandez,

00:17:10.590 --> 00:17:14.560
who used to run a local
Republican organization in Texas.

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:16.680
And here, he's quoted as
saying, "I think Trump

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:18.670
kind of hit it right on
the head for people here

00:17:18.670 --> 00:17:20.620
with the 'drain the swamp'
message, because the Valley

00:17:20.620 --> 00:17:22.130
has been riddled by corruption.

00:17:22.130 --> 00:17:23.740
People here have seen how year after year,

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:25.950
at least one elected
Democratic official locally

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:28.210
has been indicted for
bribery or corruption.

00:17:28.210 --> 00:17:30.500
I also think the riots and
calls to defund the police

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:32.870
turned off a lot of people who
are normally maybe moderate

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:34.350
or conservative Democrats

00:17:34.350 --> 00:17:35.820
and come from law enforcement families

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:38.140
who were just appalled
by that kind of message."

00:17:38.140 --> 00:17:40.830
So this is very much a
debate of campaign tactics,

00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:43.600
campaign contact, versus messaging.

00:17:43.600 --> 00:17:46.720
And we as social scientists
actually know a lot

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:49.090
about this debate and
many people on this panel

00:17:49.090 --> 00:17:51.270
have written a lot of
research on this debate.

00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:55.553
So I'm actually citing
most of the research

00:17:55.553 --> 00:17:57.180
from other panelists.

00:17:57.180 --> 00:17:59.040
And hopefully I get them right,

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:01.690
and hopefully they agree
with my interpretation.

00:18:01.690 --> 00:18:05.450
But, Professor Gerber has
done really path-breaking work

00:18:05.450 --> 00:18:08.760
on what we know about the
effect of campaign tactics

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:11.450
on the ability to increase voter turnout.

00:18:11.450 --> 00:18:12.840
And if you're interested in this topic,

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:15.797
he has a fantastic book
with Professor Green called,

00:18:15.797 --> 00:18:17.570
"Get Out the Vote," which goes through

00:18:17.570 --> 00:18:18.900
all of the social science,

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:20.150
all of the evidence that we know

00:18:20.150 --> 00:18:22.970
on ways to increase voter turnout.

00:18:22.970 --> 00:18:25.210
And what they find in this book is that,

00:18:25.210 --> 00:18:29.320
in high-salience elections,
such as presidential races,

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:32.960
for every 1,000 conversations
that a campaign has

00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:36.710
they're able to generate
about seven additional votes.

00:18:36.710 --> 00:18:39.570
And in practice, a
campaign needs to knock on

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:43.160
about three doors in order
to have one conversation.

00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:44.830
So we can do the
back-of-the-envelope math,

00:18:44.830 --> 00:18:46.400
and we can ask ourselves,

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:48.800
if Joe Biden just knocked
on more doors in Texas

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:50.860
would he have been able to win?

00:18:50.860 --> 00:18:54.160
And to kinda put the cart
before the horse answer,

00:18:54.160 --> 00:18:55.610
the answer is no.

00:18:55.610 --> 00:18:56.770
So if you go through the math,

00:18:56.770 --> 00:18:59.100
in order to have won Texas
through mobilization,

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:02.830
through increasing turnout,
Joe Biden would have needed to

00:19:02.830 --> 00:19:06.400
mobilize 630,000 additional votes.

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:07.810
And this would have required

00:19:07.810 --> 00:19:11.370
around 4.4 million conversations,
which would have required

00:19:11.370 --> 00:19:14.050
knocking on around 13.2 million doors,

00:19:14.050 --> 00:19:16.160
and unfortunately for the Biden campaign

00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:19.250
there are only about 7.5
million eligible voters in Texas

00:19:19.250 --> 00:19:21.820
who did not vote in 2020.

00:19:21.820 --> 00:19:23.840
So there just aren't
enough of these voters

00:19:23.840 --> 00:19:26.220
for additional campaign contact,

00:19:26.220 --> 00:19:28.360
additional campaign conversations

00:19:28.360 --> 00:19:30.400
to really make a meaningful difference

00:19:30.400 --> 00:19:33.580
in overturning this
particular election result.

00:19:33.580 --> 00:19:34.760
And we can think about a different way

00:19:34.760 --> 00:19:36.070
campaigns might be effective,

00:19:36.070 --> 00:19:37.800
and that is through persuasion.

00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:40.240
And this is something I've
written a little bit about.

00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:41.680
And in my research,

00:19:41.680 --> 00:19:46.560
we find that for every 1,000
conversations a campaign has,

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:49.790
they're able to persuade
around six additional votes.

00:19:49.790 --> 00:19:50.623
They're able to generate

00:19:50.623 --> 00:19:53.330
about six additional
votes through persuasion.

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:56.000
And in order to have won
Texas through persuasion,

00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:58.480
the Biden campaign
would have needed to get

00:19:58.480 --> 00:20:02.800
320,000 Trump voters to
instead become Biden voters.

00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:05.750
So we can again go through
that back-of-the-envelope math

00:20:05.750 --> 00:20:07.160
and see that this would have required

00:20:07.160 --> 00:20:11.770
around 1.9 million conversations,
5.8 million door knocks,

00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:14.650
and unfortunately, there
were only about 5.9 million

00:20:14.650 --> 00:20:16.370
Trump voters in Texas.

00:20:16.370 --> 00:20:20.630
And we can also think about
the work that professors

00:20:20.630 --> 00:20:22.970
Coppock and Vavreck, along
with Professor Hill have done

00:20:22.970 --> 00:20:25.860
on the small effects of
political advertising

00:20:25.860 --> 00:20:27.550
are small regardless of context message,

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:28.710
sender or receiver.

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:30.830
Because of these small persuasive effects

00:20:30.830 --> 00:20:32.840
there just often aren't enough voters

00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:34.070
to have a meaningful impact

00:20:34.070 --> 00:20:36.860
when it comes to presidential races.

00:20:36.860 --> 00:20:39.960
So, I think from what we know about

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:42.350
the science of campaign outreach,

00:20:42.350 --> 00:20:44.620
it's just that more campaign outreach

00:20:44.620 --> 00:20:47.780
often isn't sufficient to
change election results

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:50.730
in presidential elections in swing states.

00:20:50.730 --> 00:20:53.010
I used Texas as an example,
but we could apply this

00:20:53.010 --> 00:20:55.480
back-of-the-envelope math to other states

00:20:55.480 --> 00:20:57.550
and come up with a very
similar conclusion.

00:20:57.550 --> 00:20:59.730
There's just limited
effect of this outreach

00:20:59.730 --> 00:21:02.630
and not large enough
effects to meaningful change

00:21:02.630 --> 00:21:05.150
these presidential election results.

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:08.030
I do wanna just like raise a
very important caveat which is,

00:21:08.030 --> 00:21:10.140
if you do this kind of
work, if you volunteer,

00:21:10.140 --> 00:21:12.340
if you work for an organization
who does this kind of work,

00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:15.790
you're efforts still can
make a very big difference

00:21:15.790 --> 00:21:17.930
because some elections are really close

00:21:17.930 --> 00:21:20.010
and the evidence that we have is that

00:21:20.010 --> 00:21:22.230
there are larger effects
in primaries, local races,

00:21:22.230 --> 00:21:23.063
and ballot measures.

00:21:23.063 --> 00:21:25.310
So if you do this kind of volunteer work,

00:21:25.310 --> 00:21:27.410
double down in those types
of primaries, local races,

00:21:27.410 --> 00:21:29.670
and ballot measures, 'cause that's where

00:21:29.670 --> 00:21:33.360
the back-of-the-envelope
math is much more favorable.

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:36.270
So what does matter in
presidential campaigns

00:21:36.270 --> 00:21:38.440
if it's not additional campaign contact,

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:39.940
additional campaign outreach?

00:21:39.940 --> 00:21:42.640
And here I want to point to
a really influential book

00:21:42.640 --> 00:21:46.490
that Professor Vavreck wrote
called "The Message Matters."

00:21:46.490 --> 00:21:49.160
And Professor Vavreck isn't
paying me to say this.

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:50.480
This is not a paid endorsement,

00:21:50.480 --> 00:21:52.520
but this is a really influential book,

00:21:52.520 --> 00:21:53.353
and if you're interested

00:21:53.353 --> 00:21:55.120
in understanding presidential campaigns,

00:21:55.120 --> 00:21:57.290
I highly recommend checkin' it out.

00:21:57.290 --> 00:22:00.700
And the title does a nice
job capturing what matters.

00:22:00.700 --> 00:22:03.270
It's presidential, it's in the messaging.

00:22:03.270 --> 00:22:06.720
And Professor Vavreck
lays out some predictions

00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:10.350
as to what kind of messaging
campaigns ought to be using.

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:12.210
And she talks about two situations,

00:22:12.210 --> 00:22:15.080
one where the economy is good,
one where the economy is bad.

00:22:15.080 --> 00:22:17.457
And when the economy is good,
we should expect the incumbent

00:22:17.457 --> 00:22:21.110
should focus his or her
campaign message on the economy,

00:22:21.110 --> 00:22:24.010
championing the fact
that the economy is good

00:22:24.010 --> 00:22:25.850
and thus you should
reelect this candidate.

00:22:25.850 --> 00:22:27.570
And for the challenger,

00:22:27.570 --> 00:22:29.569
they obviously don't want
to talk about the economy,

00:22:29.569 --> 00:22:31.410
because that helps the incumbent.

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:33.240
Instead, they should focus
their campaign message

00:22:33.240 --> 00:22:35.460
on some other popular issue

00:22:35.460 --> 00:22:37.930
where they're more popular
than the incumbent.

00:22:37.930 --> 00:22:40.510
The incumbent is stuck
in an unpopular position

00:22:40.510 --> 00:22:43.850
and this other issue is
or can be made important.

00:22:43.850 --> 00:22:44.990
And when the economy is bad

00:22:44.990 --> 00:22:47.160
we would expect the opposite prediction.

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:49.630
And Professor Vavreck has
a really nice conclusion

00:22:49.630 --> 00:22:52.160
where she says, "Candidates who violate

00:22:52.160 --> 00:22:55.350
these prescriptions, these
predictions lose elections."

00:22:55.350 --> 00:22:59.810
And by inverse, when they
follow these prescriptions

00:22:59.810 --> 00:23:01.230
they win elections.

00:23:01.230 --> 00:23:04.420
So it's really about, not these
individual campaign tactics,

00:23:04.420 --> 00:23:07.230
but these broader meta-narrative
points about the messaging.

00:23:07.230 --> 00:23:09.420
About are you focusing on the economy,

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:11.480
if the economy's good
and you're incumbent.

00:23:11.480 --> 00:23:13.670
Or are you focusing on
some other popular issue,

00:23:13.670 --> 00:23:15.430
if you can't talk about the economy.

00:23:15.430 --> 00:23:17.347
And this idea of messaging
really needs to be

00:23:17.347 --> 00:23:19.550
talked more about and taken seriously.

00:23:19.550 --> 00:23:20.580
And I think really helps us

00:23:20.580 --> 00:23:22.410
understand presidential election results.

00:23:22.410 --> 00:23:23.243
So thank you all so much

00:23:23.243 --> 00:23:26.044
and I look forward to
the rest of the panel.

00:23:26.044 --> 00:23:27.383
- Thank you.

00:23:27.383 --> 00:23:32.383
I have to say that really feel
a little bit out of place,

00:23:33.040 --> 00:23:38.040
as I come to this from a
somewhat different perspective,

00:23:39.060 --> 00:23:43.230
in being very much about
the practice of politics.

00:23:43.230 --> 00:23:47.040
And the odd thing in listening
to the last two speakers

00:23:47.040 --> 00:23:49.560
is that I may actually believe

00:23:49.560 --> 00:23:52.753
that campaigns generally even matter less.

00:23:54.560 --> 00:23:56.556
Even though that's what I've spent like,

00:23:56.556 --> 00:23:59.763
as Alan said, 300 decades doing.

00:24:03.478 --> 00:24:04.610
And I really wanted to take on

00:24:04.610 --> 00:24:06.640
a lot of what was just
said, but instead I'll do

00:24:06.640 --> 00:24:09.520
what I was gonna start out doing.

00:24:09.520 --> 00:24:13.543
Which is sort of do a sort of
broader sweep on all of this.

00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:19.500
So, imagine that United
States were 100 people.

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:22.450
And on November 8th, 2016,

00:24:22.450 --> 00:24:26.110
51 of them voted for Hillary Clinton,

00:24:26.110 --> 00:24:28.363
49 of them voted for Donald Trump.

00:24:29.350 --> 00:24:33.440
You then had a quarter
million people dead of COVID,

00:24:33.440 --> 00:24:36.940
30 million people were unemployed,
racial justice movement,

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:39.800
his impeachment, all of that stuff.

00:24:39.800 --> 00:24:44.800
You end up outspending Trump
by 3:2 with $14 billion,

00:24:45.530 --> 00:24:49.650
and one X moves from the Republican side

00:24:49.650 --> 00:24:51.090
to the Democratic side.

00:24:51.090 --> 00:24:53.570
That's it, that's what happened
over the last four years.

00:24:53.570 --> 00:24:55.620
That is it, right.

00:24:55.620 --> 00:24:57.560
You're in a room with 100 people

00:24:57.560 --> 00:24:59.280
and what happened was one person

00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:01.590
crossed the other side of the room,

00:25:01.590 --> 00:25:03.183
after spending $14 billion.

00:25:05.750 --> 00:25:08.280
So, how'd that happen?

00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:12.100
We saw unprecedented political engagement.

00:25:12.100 --> 00:25:16.300
Four years, as I said, of
like insane dislocation.

00:25:16.300 --> 00:25:18.960
Uncertainty that none of
us had really experienced

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:20.270
in our lifetimes.

00:25:20.270 --> 00:25:22.943
And almost no change in
our political choices.

00:25:23.780 --> 00:25:27.030
Which has to lead to kind
of epistemological angst.

00:25:27.030 --> 00:25:30.030
Like we don't know what the
heck is happening in this world.

00:25:30.920 --> 00:25:32.690
So, just to run through that.

00:25:32.690 --> 00:25:34.648
To really get a sense, this is,

00:25:34.648 --> 00:25:37.800
the level of increase in civic engagement

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:40.250
is like hasn't happened.

00:25:40.250 --> 00:25:41.780
It just hasn't happened.

00:25:41.780 --> 00:25:45.380
The last two cycles, if
you look on the right,

00:25:45.380 --> 00:25:47.980
it's things like people
coming home from World War II

00:25:47.980 --> 00:25:52.280
that saw the same kind
of increase in turnout.

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:55.520
Trump is like a political noise machine.

00:25:55.520 --> 00:25:57.920
He makes every election the Superbowl.

00:25:57.920 --> 00:26:01.600
And what that means is that
just like the Superbowl

00:26:01.600 --> 00:26:03.920
people who don't even watch
a game during the season

00:26:03.920 --> 00:26:05.670
show up for the party.

00:26:05.670 --> 00:26:07.477
And that's what happened in '18

00:26:07.477 --> 00:26:09.210
and that's what happened now.

00:26:09.210 --> 00:26:11.773
It's like incredible engagement.

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:16.600
He's the most unpopular
president in a way that again,

00:26:16.600 --> 00:26:20.330
you just have to really see
stuff like this to comprehend.

00:26:20.330 --> 00:26:23.940
The only president who never had a month

00:26:23.940 --> 00:26:25.670
where he was above the water.

00:26:25.670 --> 00:26:28.220
There hasn't been a
period since he announced

00:26:28.220 --> 00:26:31.020
that more people wanted him
to be president than didn't.

00:26:31.960 --> 00:26:36.380
That his approval ratings,
despite all this turmoil,

00:26:36.380 --> 00:26:41.050
stayed with a standard deviation,
that other than Eisenhower

00:26:41.050 --> 00:26:44.010
is unbelievable, completely out of whack.

00:26:44.010 --> 00:26:48.490
And the structure of the
race never changed from 2016.

00:26:48.490 --> 00:26:51.630
It's like the people were
organized around Trump,

00:26:51.630 --> 00:26:52.530
that was it.

00:26:52.530 --> 00:26:55.700
People who had voted for
one of the two in '16,

00:26:55.700 --> 00:26:58.810
not many of them moved and the
movement was always toward,

00:26:58.810 --> 00:27:02.270
a little bit of movement
net towards the Democrats

00:27:02.270 --> 00:27:05.560
because there was a Lincoln
Project, no Roosevelt Project.

00:27:05.560 --> 00:27:07.610
The Johnson, Stein voters
there wasn't a poll

00:27:07.610 --> 00:27:12.430
where the Democratic
margin was less than 10.

00:27:12.430 --> 00:27:14.350
2018 midterm voters, 20 points.

00:27:14.350 --> 00:27:16.540
So like that should have fueled our sense

00:27:16.540 --> 00:27:18.850
that the people were
moving away from Trump

00:27:18.850 --> 00:27:21.963
to a greater extent, which lead
to these kinda crazy things.

00:27:23.441 --> 00:27:26.820
You know, with 90% chance he's
gonna win 347 electoral votes

00:27:27.670 --> 00:27:32.283
more than twice as many
popular vote margin.

00:27:33.757 --> 00:27:34.850
I'm just putting the numbers up here,

00:27:34.850 --> 00:27:37.990
so like I'm shut, like have some semblance

00:27:37.990 --> 00:27:39.150
I'm not making this up.

00:27:39.150 --> 00:27:40.780
Basic thought experiment.

00:27:40.780 --> 00:27:44.110
And the first row's just
like the 2016 election.

00:27:44.110 --> 00:27:47.290
This pretty much, for three
of the folks on the panel,

00:27:47.290 --> 00:27:49.830
who like read me writing
about this ahead of time,

00:27:49.830 --> 00:27:51.833
this isn't like post talk.

00:27:53.877 --> 00:27:58.877
So basically you have the 2016
voters were 2.2 for Clinton.

00:28:00.590 --> 00:28:04.510
People who voted in
2018, were 22 points for

00:28:04.510 --> 00:28:07.740
the House Democrats, which
meant people who came back

00:28:07.740 --> 00:28:12.190
from the '16, were five points Democratic,

00:28:12.190 --> 00:28:15.230
and overall it was a 7.4 point.

00:28:15.230 --> 00:28:18.420
Everything like completely
consistent with the story

00:28:18.420 --> 00:28:19.750
all of us wanted to know.

00:28:19.750 --> 00:28:20.977
Which is people were done with Trump

00:28:20.977 --> 00:28:23.300
and moving away, all that.

00:28:23.300 --> 00:28:25.440
Which made the polls seem more reasonable.

00:28:25.440 --> 00:28:27.400
So I did a thing where I did it two ways.

00:28:27.400 --> 00:28:29.660
One is assume that all the people

00:28:29.660 --> 00:28:32.250
who voted in 2016 really
didn't change their mind.

00:28:32.250 --> 00:28:33.450
And if they didn't change their mind

00:28:33.450 --> 00:28:36.300
and the people who showed
up in 2018 didn't show up,

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:39.610
then the 2020 election
should be 4.5 spread,

00:28:39.610 --> 00:28:41.060
which is what it actually is.

00:28:42.100 --> 00:28:47.100
Or the people who showed up
then were 5.1 for Democrats,

00:28:48.040 --> 00:28:51.770
were actually reflective of
Clinton voters, 2016 voters,

00:28:51.770 --> 00:28:53.620
and it should have been seven points.

00:28:55.610 --> 00:28:57.260
It should actually be a little bit less

00:28:57.260 --> 00:28:59.310
if you do fancier math,

00:28:59.310 --> 00:29:00.760
but that's basically
the order of magnitude.

00:29:00.760 --> 00:29:02.700
So the polls were like,
which were above that,

00:29:02.700 --> 00:29:05.940
like we're living in a
world that couldn't exist.

00:29:05.940 --> 00:29:07.710
But that was sort of what was goin' on.

00:29:07.710 --> 00:29:09.943
I thought ahead of time
six was gonna be it.

00:29:10.870 --> 00:29:12.733
But that's sort of what was real.

00:29:14.930 --> 00:29:17.310
But that fed our
willingness to believe polls

00:29:17.310 --> 00:29:20.060
that were telling us something
very, very different.

00:29:20.060 --> 00:29:21.710
Because we've just seen Democrats

00:29:21.710 --> 00:29:23.780
win these big victories in 2018.

00:29:23.780 --> 00:29:26.583
We saw polling that we liked and all that.

00:29:27.430 --> 00:29:30.040
But I would argue that
polling can't be fixed at all

00:29:30.040 --> 00:29:32.150
when Trump is on the ballot,

00:29:32.150 --> 00:29:36.660
because there are no shy Trump voters.

00:29:36.660 --> 00:29:39.100
In fact, if you get in a room with one

00:29:39.100 --> 00:29:40.983
that seems like a silly concept.

00:29:42.922 --> 00:29:44.120
MAGA hats the whole thing.

00:29:44.120 --> 00:29:45.710
Who are they?

00:29:45.710 --> 00:29:47.730
But they don't like to take polls.

00:29:47.730 --> 00:29:50.777
And not because necessarily they think

00:29:50.777 --> 00:29:53.060
they're a Democratic plot,

00:29:53.060 --> 00:29:54.840
but that's just not who they are.

00:29:54.840 --> 00:29:59.270
So imagine, Dave the
backhoe driver in Michigan,

00:29:59.270 --> 00:30:00.900
who's never thought that there was

00:30:00.900 --> 00:30:02.820
a candidate like Trump to vote for before

00:30:02.820 --> 00:30:04.500
and never showed up.

00:30:04.500 --> 00:30:08.080
When he decides, "Wow,
Trump's the real deal,

00:30:08.080 --> 00:30:09.980
I'm gonna go out and vote."

00:30:09.980 --> 00:30:13.210
He doesn't realize that he also
has to get on a YouGov panel

00:30:13.210 --> 00:30:15.370
or a Lucid panel, or something like that

00:30:15.370 --> 00:30:18.500
so the rest of us know that
he's part of the electorate

00:30:18.500 --> 00:30:20.850
and is gonna turn out in 2020.

00:30:20.850 --> 00:30:23.490
Which is why the polls
were right on in 2018,

00:30:23.490 --> 00:30:25.343
not on at all in 2020.

00:30:27.150 --> 00:30:29.670
But that's a lot of what's going on.

00:30:29.670 --> 00:30:30.620
And you can't fix that,

00:30:30.620 --> 00:30:32.730
because in 2016 people wanted to believe,

00:30:32.730 --> 00:30:34.470
it was this white, non-college thing.

00:30:34.470 --> 00:30:35.490
But I don't have enough time to say

00:30:35.490 --> 00:30:38.200
why that's really pernicious
and wrong and bad.

00:30:38.200 --> 00:30:40.590
But it's not right, it didn't work.

00:30:40.590 --> 00:30:44.540
It's really just people who
like Trump do not like polls.

00:30:44.540 --> 00:30:46.970
That correlation it's
not a demographic thing,

00:30:46.970 --> 00:30:49.750
it's just who they are and all that.

00:30:49.750 --> 00:30:50.630
And the second thing though,

00:30:50.630 --> 00:30:53.070
which is in some ways more important,

00:30:53.070 --> 00:30:55.100
is that the polls failed to do that,

00:30:55.100 --> 00:30:57.733
but we also failed to
just use common sense.

00:30:59.390 --> 00:31:01.560
For people that really
are engaged in politics

00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:02.690
and believed it.

00:31:02.690 --> 00:31:05.710
Because it's like we've
become sort of a society

00:31:05.710 --> 00:31:08.900
that wants to know what the weather is

00:31:08.900 --> 00:31:11.340
and checks our app
instead of going outside

00:31:11.340 --> 00:31:12.690
and seeing what's goin' on.

00:31:13.630 --> 00:31:15.000
And that's what the polling was.

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:17.280
We were just like looking at these stats

00:31:17.280 --> 00:31:18.850
without like actually going out

00:31:18.850 --> 00:31:20.830
and looking at what was
happening in the world.

00:31:20.830 --> 00:31:24.920
So again, have the receipts,
over the summer just thought,

00:31:24.920 --> 00:31:25.753
well this is not right.

00:31:25.753 --> 00:31:27.130
Looked at two things.

00:31:27.130 --> 00:31:28.920
One was voter registration.

00:31:28.920 --> 00:31:31.570
White non-college off the chart.

00:31:31.570 --> 00:31:34.269
Democratic base groups depressed.

00:31:34.269 --> 00:31:36.310
A clear signal what was coming.

00:31:36.310 --> 00:31:37.960
Then you get to early vote.

00:31:37.960 --> 00:31:41.360
Big campaign to get Democratic
base voters to vote early.

00:31:41.360 --> 00:31:43.600
No campaign to get white, non-college.

00:31:43.600 --> 00:31:46.950
White, non-college among
non-voters is keeping pace

00:31:46.950 --> 00:31:49.720
or exceeding Democratic base groups,

00:31:49.720 --> 00:31:53.440
who had $200 million thrown
at them to go vote by mail.

00:31:53.440 --> 00:31:55.270
All of which is saying, polls

00:31:55.270 --> 00:31:57.920
that are saying the opposite
can't possibly be right.

00:31:59.030 --> 00:32:02.120
And so that basically
leaves you in a place where

00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:05.010
you just can't depend on the polls.

00:32:05.010 --> 00:32:08.120
And I think I'm outta
time, but to wrap up,

00:32:08.120 --> 00:32:09.310
if you really step back

00:32:09.310 --> 00:32:12.070
and were like clicking too
much on FiveThirtyEight

00:32:12.070 --> 00:32:14.790
or reading all these, all of this stuff

00:32:14.790 --> 00:32:17.470
was narrating noise in the polls.

00:32:17.470 --> 00:32:20.420
We were getting stories about
seniors doing this and that,

00:32:20.420 --> 00:32:21.920
doing this and all of that,

00:32:21.920 --> 00:32:24.100
to just to account for statistical noise

00:32:24.100 --> 00:32:25.263
and for these errors.

00:32:26.190 --> 00:32:29.970
If as a practitioner
on November 9th, 2016,

00:32:29.970 --> 00:32:33.880
I knew Michigan, Pennsylvania,
Wisconsin, Arizona

00:32:33.880 --> 00:32:36.900
were gonna be key, but
Nevada was or wasn't,

00:32:36.900 --> 00:32:40.210
didn't see Georgia,
didn't need a single poll

00:32:40.210 --> 00:32:42.113
for the next four years.

00:32:42.990 --> 00:32:45.020
And if I had them, I
thought Florida was in play.

00:32:45.020 --> 00:32:46.940
I thought North Carolina was in play.

00:32:46.940 --> 00:32:49.690
All of these things that were illusions.

00:32:49.690 --> 00:32:50.970
So I think that's time.

00:32:50.970 --> 00:32:52.423
And I'll stop there.

