I think I am next.

00:32:59.690 --> 00:33:03.453
And I'm also gonna share
some visuals with you.

00:33:04.310 --> 00:33:08.200
It is a pleasure to be
here, virtually in New Haven

00:33:08.200 --> 00:33:12.310
with all of these people from
whom I have learned so much

00:33:12.310 --> 00:33:13.990
over my career.

00:33:13.990 --> 00:33:16.790
And I'm gonna just talk briefly

00:33:16.790 --> 00:33:21.003
about how I think about
what happened in 2020.

00:33:21.970 --> 00:33:25.670
So, I like to think about
presidential elections

00:33:25.670 --> 00:33:27.810
as having two components.

00:33:27.810 --> 00:33:31.960
The big things, and these are
the things that always matter

00:33:31.960 --> 00:33:35.130
every presidential election,
they affect the vote choice.

00:33:35.130 --> 00:33:37.780
These are things like
party identification,

00:33:37.780 --> 00:33:39.410
the state of the nation's economy.

00:33:39.410 --> 00:33:42.580
Josh has told you a little
bit about my messaging work

00:33:42.580 --> 00:33:43.413
surrounding the economy.

00:33:43.413 --> 00:33:45.740
I wanna talk about that a little more.

00:33:45.740 --> 00:33:47.410
So that's the first set.

00:33:47.410 --> 00:33:49.040
And the second set are the things

00:33:49.040 --> 00:33:51.700
that I call the swing things.

00:33:51.700 --> 00:33:55.290
So things that happen
maybe in one election,

00:33:55.290 --> 00:33:56.890
but not in another election.

00:33:56.890 --> 00:33:59.130
This might who the candidates are.

00:33:59.130 --> 00:34:01.980
It might the messaging that
they choose to focus on.

00:34:01.980 --> 00:34:04.800
It might be whether there's
a global pandemic happening,

00:34:04.800 --> 00:34:06.970
whether there's a global financial crisis.

00:34:06.970 --> 00:34:09.710
So, things that you can't count on

00:34:09.710 --> 00:34:11.620
happening every single year.

00:34:11.620 --> 00:34:13.917
So big things and swing things.

00:34:13.917 --> 00:34:17.500
And we're gonna talk about
a few things in each set

00:34:17.500 --> 00:34:19.420
in the next couple of minutes.

00:34:19.420 --> 00:34:23.270
So the first big thing I
mentioned is party identification.

00:34:23.270 --> 00:34:24.470
And the way that I want everyone

00:34:24.470 --> 00:34:26.140
to think about party identification

00:34:26.140 --> 00:34:29.070
is that is strong and stable.

00:34:29.070 --> 00:34:32.370
Strong because most people in the country,

00:34:32.370 --> 00:34:34.960
most people who turn
up to vote in elections

00:34:34.960 --> 00:34:37.720
have a party identification
with one of the two

00:34:37.720 --> 00:34:40.090
major American political parties.

00:34:40.090 --> 00:34:45.090
Maybe 10%, somewhere around
10% of the electorate,

00:34:45.390 --> 00:34:48.140
probably less than that,
will consider themselves

00:34:48.140 --> 00:34:50.300
to be truly independent of party,

00:34:50.300 --> 00:34:53.210
and not even say they
lean when you push them.

00:34:53.210 --> 00:34:56.320
So almost everyone has
a party identification.

00:34:56.320 --> 00:34:59.920
And then conditional on
having one of those party IDs,

00:34:59.920 --> 00:35:01.950
people pretty much vote for the candidate

00:35:01.950 --> 00:35:03.610
that is in their party.

00:35:03.610 --> 00:35:07.150
So for example, in 2020
about 86% of people

00:35:07.150 --> 00:35:09.870
who call themselves Republicans
voted for Donald Trump.

00:35:09.870 --> 00:35:12.670
90% of people who call
themselves Democrats,

00:35:12.670 --> 00:35:15.470
oh sorry, that should
say voted for Joe Biden,

00:35:15.470 --> 00:35:17.630
not Hillary Clinton.

00:35:17.630 --> 00:35:22.630
So the robust effect of party
identification on vote choice.

00:35:22.860 --> 00:35:24.500
So those numbers are really big.

00:35:24.500 --> 00:35:26.790
They're close to 90%, they are like that

00:35:26.790 --> 00:35:28.760
every presidential election,

00:35:28.760 --> 00:35:31.740
plus or minus two or three points.

00:35:31.740 --> 00:35:34.250
That's a lotta people
doing what we can predict

00:35:34.250 --> 00:35:37.300
they're gonna do, a long time in advance.

00:35:37.300 --> 00:35:40.070
People's party
identifications rarely change.

00:35:40.070 --> 00:35:42.170
So, if you were a Republican last time,

00:35:42.170 --> 00:35:44.530
you're probably still
a Republican this time.

00:35:44.530 --> 00:35:46.950
So that means that the number of people

00:35:46.950 --> 00:35:51.170
who can swing elections is a small set,

00:35:51.170 --> 00:35:52.950
but it's an important and pivotal set.

00:35:52.950 --> 00:35:54.600
And so we wanna think about that.

00:35:55.470 --> 00:35:58.760
Okay, a second big factor
that matters every year

00:35:58.760 --> 00:36:01.320
is the state of the nation's economy.

00:36:01.320 --> 00:36:04.960
And as Josh mentioned earlier,
I have spent a lot of time

00:36:04.960 --> 00:36:07.410
thinking about how important this is

00:36:07.410 --> 00:36:09.570
in every presidential election

00:36:09.570 --> 00:36:12.890
and how it sets the stage
for presidential campaigns.

00:36:12.890 --> 00:36:15.430
It isn't the play, but
it's the stage dressing.

00:36:15.430 --> 00:36:19.330
It tells you whether we're
watching a comedy or a drama.

00:36:19.330 --> 00:36:20.620
So what I'm showing you here

00:36:20.620 --> 00:36:22.130
is just a little bit of evidence

00:36:22.130 --> 00:36:27.050
about how robust the effect
of the nation's economy is

00:36:27.050 --> 00:36:29.740
on presidential election outcomes.

00:36:29.740 --> 00:36:33.700
On the horizontal axis
here, I'm showing you change

00:36:33.700 --> 00:36:38.170
in the nation's economy,
GDP growth let's call it.

00:36:38.170 --> 00:36:39.930
In the first six months
of an election year,

00:36:39.930 --> 00:36:43.140
January to June of every election year,

00:36:43.140 --> 00:36:46.900
the year markers here are
plotting symbols indicating

00:36:46.900 --> 00:36:48.770
the presidential election.

00:36:48.770 --> 00:36:52.810
On the Y axis, I'm showing you
the incumbent party's share

00:36:52.810 --> 00:36:55.920
of the two-party vote in these elections.

00:36:55.920 --> 00:36:57.330
And I've drawn a line here,

00:36:57.330 --> 00:36:59.610
that's sort of the line of best fit

00:36:59.610 --> 00:37:01.730
through these plotting symbols.

00:37:01.730 --> 00:37:04.410
And you can see that it slopes upward.

00:37:04.410 --> 00:37:05.550
What does that tell us?

00:37:05.550 --> 00:37:09.160
It tells us that as GDP is increasing

00:37:09.160 --> 00:37:10.770
in the six months of an election year,

00:37:10.770 --> 00:37:14.300
so growth is positive,
incumbent parties do better.

00:37:14.300 --> 00:37:18.200
When growth is negative,
incumbent parties do worse.

00:37:18.200 --> 00:37:21.610
So, incumbent parties in
growing economies typically win

00:37:21.610 --> 00:37:25.210
and incumbent parties in shrinking
economies typically lose.

00:37:25.210 --> 00:37:27.910
And I've highlighted here 2020 for you.

00:37:27.910 --> 00:37:30.580
If you're curious, right here is 2016,

00:37:30.580 --> 00:37:34.180
almost exactly on the prediction line.

00:37:34.180 --> 00:37:37.920
Here's 2020, and you can see Trump

00:37:37.920 --> 00:37:40.380
is over-performing his economy,

00:37:40.380 --> 00:37:44.740
but it is the worst economy,
in terms of GDP growth,

00:37:44.740 --> 00:37:46.890
that we've seen since The New Deal.

00:37:46.890 --> 00:37:51.890
So, out here with 1980 and
Jimmy Carter's reelection.

00:37:51.890 --> 00:37:53.760
But 2020 is kind of unique.

00:37:53.760 --> 00:37:56.603
And I wanna show you a
little bit about why.

00:37:57.640 --> 00:38:00.940
The state of nation's economy
is a little complicated

00:38:00.940 --> 00:38:03.350
because of the stimulus payments.

00:38:03.350 --> 00:38:06.320
So, on the left side here is
the graph I just showed you.

00:38:06.320 --> 00:38:07.980
This is GDP growth.

00:38:07.980 --> 00:38:12.980
But on the right-hand side,
same Y axis, same vertical axis,

00:38:13.000 --> 00:38:14.860
but now I'm showing you growth rate

00:38:14.860 --> 00:38:18.853
of real disposable income
per capita, January to June.

00:38:20.646 --> 00:38:21.823
You see the same trend.

00:38:22.930 --> 00:38:25.090
RDI goes up, people have
more money in their pockets,

00:38:25.090 --> 00:38:27.220
it's better for the incumbent.

00:38:27.220 --> 00:38:32.143
But here's 2020, it is now
the best-performing RDI

00:38:33.270 --> 00:38:35.870
economic election since The New Deal.

00:38:35.870 --> 00:38:39.000
So 2020 presents this national context,

00:38:39.000 --> 00:38:41.230
this economic context to people,

00:38:41.230 --> 00:38:46.230
where the nation's economy
is shrinking and quickly,

00:38:46.350 --> 00:38:50.810
and in a very sort of scary way,

00:38:50.810 --> 00:38:54.730
but in terms of how much
money people have available

00:38:54.730 --> 00:38:57.300
they have more than in any previous

00:38:57.300 --> 00:38:59.500
presidential election year.

00:38:59.500 --> 00:39:02.913
So this is a little bit
of a unique situation.

00:39:03.932 --> 00:39:08.900
If you look at 1980, here's
the other bad growth year

00:39:08.900 --> 00:39:12.620
and RDI is also shrinking.

00:39:12.620 --> 00:39:14.270
Here's global financial crisis.

00:39:14.270 --> 00:39:18.260
So it's unusual to have an
election that is the worst

00:39:18.260 --> 00:39:21.840
and the best on two different
economic indicators.

00:39:21.840 --> 00:39:23.480
So that's gonna maybe complicate

