00:46:47.320 --> 00:46:50.137
- So, there are a few
questions about polling

00:46:50.137 --> 00:46:51.520
and the accuracy of polling.

00:46:51.520 --> 00:46:54.820
And so I wanted just to ask the panelists

00:46:54.820 --> 00:46:55.993
to comment on that.

00:46:57.030 --> 00:46:58.980
I think Alex alluded to the fact

00:46:58.980 --> 00:47:03.980
that there's a lot of weighting
going on in the polling

00:47:04.450 --> 00:47:07.510
and alluded to response rate issues.

00:47:07.510 --> 00:47:12.510
I don't know that everyone
knows what the response rates

00:47:13.310 --> 00:47:14.430
are on polls.

00:47:14.430 --> 00:47:18.240
One thing I say sometimes to folks is

00:47:18.240 --> 00:47:21.220
do you remember if you had a very good

00:47:21.220 --> 00:47:24.740
high school statistics
course you learned about

00:47:24.740 --> 00:47:29.460
the properties of a random
sample from a large population

00:47:29.460 --> 00:47:32.980
and how if you pull a
relatively small random sample

00:47:32.980 --> 00:47:35.780
from a large population,
you're still able to get

00:47:35.780 --> 00:47:39.640
very accurate estimates of
the underlying properties

00:47:39.640 --> 00:47:41.570
of that large population.

00:47:41.570 --> 00:47:46.350
And so, when you hear about
the errors in the polls

00:47:46.350 --> 00:47:50.210
reported in the media, that
has absolutely nothing to do

00:47:50.210 --> 00:47:54.020
with that model you learned
as a high school student.

00:47:54.020 --> 00:47:59.020
You're not getting a random
sample from a population.

00:47:59.100 --> 00:48:02.840
It's really you're getting
a lot of weighting going on

00:48:02.840 --> 00:48:05.550
to a relatively small number of responses.

00:48:05.550 --> 00:48:09.610
But if you could explain
Alex what you think

00:48:09.610 --> 00:48:12.051
about the response rates and the weighting

00:48:12.051 --> 00:48:14.470
and what role you think that played

00:48:14.470 --> 00:48:17.803
in the issues with the
polling in this election.

00:48:20.520 --> 00:48:23.610
- So, as I said, I'm not a polling expert.

00:48:23.610 --> 00:48:26.770
I understand it on this level.

00:48:26.770 --> 00:48:29.860
The people who respond
to polls are different

00:48:29.860 --> 00:48:31.789
from the electorate.

00:48:31.789 --> 00:48:33.100
We have two problems.

00:48:33.100 --> 00:48:35.050
We don't know who they want to vote for

00:48:35.050 --> 00:48:36.980
and we don't know whether
they're going to vote.

00:48:36.980 --> 00:48:39.680
So then we ask people, "Who
are you gonna vote for?

00:48:39.680 --> 00:48:41.580
And are you going to turn out to vote."

00:48:41.580 --> 00:48:45.440
And then we need to say,
"You're a kind of person

00:48:45.440 --> 00:48:46.970
of this class.

00:48:46.970 --> 00:48:49.790
We know that there are
so many of that class

00:48:49.790 --> 00:48:51.110
in the population.

00:48:51.110 --> 00:48:54.180
And so then, we're going
to multiply your answer

00:48:54.180 --> 00:48:56.680
by the weight that we think you exist

00:48:56.680 --> 00:48:58.870
in the overall population.

00:48:58.870 --> 00:49:01.440
So we're gonna up-weight
you by the proportion

00:49:01.440 --> 00:49:03.787
of people who are like
you in the population."

00:49:03.787 --> 00:49:05.433
And so we've got this problem.

00:49:06.480 --> 00:49:10.040
Do we know the kinds of
people that there are?

00:49:10.040 --> 00:49:11.620
And I think Mike's point,

00:49:11.620 --> 00:49:15.612
which is just really interesting here.

00:49:15.612 --> 00:49:19.640
What's the problem is that
there's a type that we don't have

00:49:19.640 --> 00:49:22.860
which is the Trump voter
who doesn't take polls.

00:49:22.860 --> 00:49:25.260
And that person, first
of all, we don't know

00:49:25.260 --> 00:49:26.450
how many of them there are.

00:49:26.450 --> 00:49:28.710
So it's difficult to obtain the amount

00:49:28.710 --> 00:49:31.300
by which we need to multiply the thing.

00:49:31.300 --> 00:49:33.950
And the ones that we are
able to get on the phone,

00:49:33.950 --> 00:49:35.940
or able to get to take the YouGov panel,

00:49:35.940 --> 00:49:40.130
might be unlike their brethren who won't.

00:49:40.130 --> 00:49:42.780
And so we don't know what to re-weight by.

00:49:42.780 --> 00:49:45.020
So Mike also alluded to this idea

00:49:45.020 --> 00:49:47.150
that we need to weight by education.

00:49:47.150 --> 00:49:50.250
That was the big explanation
for the 2016 polling failure

00:49:50.250 --> 00:49:53.340
is that we were weighting
by demographic variables

00:49:53.340 --> 00:49:56.060
like age and gender.

00:49:56.060 --> 00:49:58.030
What we needed to be weighting by was

00:49:58.030 --> 00:50:00.330
how educated the person, basically
we weren't getting enough

00:50:00.330 --> 00:50:02.350
people who had low levels of education.

00:50:02.350 --> 00:50:04.940
So, gonna take the people who
had low levels of education,

00:50:04.940 --> 00:50:06.410
raise their weights up in the survey

00:50:06.410 --> 00:50:08.680
and then we would get the right answer.

00:50:08.680 --> 00:50:09.830
Well, we did that this round

00:50:09.830 --> 00:50:11.380
and we did not get the right answer.

00:50:11.380 --> 00:50:15.160
So, I think Mike's almost
like tautologically right,

00:50:15.160 --> 00:50:19.950
evidently we didn't get
the Trump supporters.

00:50:19.950 --> 00:50:22.060
And this is the big problem to solve.

00:50:22.060 --> 00:50:23.543
How do we fix that problem?

00:50:25.090 --> 00:50:29.340
- Lynn, you've had a lot
of experience in polling.

00:50:29.340 --> 00:50:31.480
I'd love to hear from
Mike, but before we do that

00:50:31.480 --> 00:50:33.910
I'd like to hear from you.

00:50:33.910 --> 00:50:36.130
What kind of response rates

00:50:36.130 --> 00:50:39.745
are we getting these day for polling?

00:50:39.745 --> 00:50:40.680
'Cause that gives us a sense

00:50:40.680 --> 00:50:42.863
of what the role of weighting is.

00:50:43.960 --> 00:50:45.160
- Well, they're super low.

00:50:45.160 --> 00:50:46.890
They're super low.

00:50:46.890 --> 00:50:51.890
A traditional, random,
digit-dial telephone poll,

00:50:52.240 --> 00:50:53.850
which I don't know if
anybody does that anymore,

00:50:53.850 --> 00:50:57.380
but those kinds of not
sampling off a list.

00:50:57.380 --> 00:51:00.980
So you're just tryin' to reach
people in some random way.

00:51:00.980 --> 00:51:03.100
Those are gonna be in
the low single digits.

00:51:03.100 --> 00:51:04.970
If you're list-based sampling

00:51:04.970 --> 00:51:06.950
you can be a little higher than that.

00:51:06.950 --> 00:51:11.950
But nobody is getting
50%, no one's getting 30%.

00:51:14.198 --> 00:51:16.460
Those are miracle numbers.

00:51:16.460 --> 00:51:18.610
And so everybody is compensating.

00:51:18.610 --> 00:51:20.840
And I'll just say two other things.

00:51:20.840 --> 00:51:24.970
One is you really want
to, people are weighting

00:51:24.970 --> 00:51:29.960
to 2016 vote, which we
know the outcome of 2016.

00:51:29.960 --> 00:51:33.300
And so these pollsters
are adjusting the sample

00:51:33.300 --> 00:51:36.470
so that it gives them the outcome of 2016.

00:51:36.470 --> 00:51:39.400
And they're still wrong in 2020.

00:51:39.400 --> 00:51:43.160
So this isn't just that we're
not finding Trump voters.

00:51:43.160 --> 00:51:44.330
We're finding Republicans,

00:51:44.330 --> 00:51:46.130
we're finding Republicans
who voted for Trump,

00:51:46.130 --> 00:51:48.610
we're making the sample give us

00:51:48.610 --> 00:51:50.480
exactly what the outcome was in 2016,

00:51:50.480 --> 00:51:52.440
and it's still off by a lot.

00:51:52.440 --> 00:51:54.610
And so I think a huge culprit here is

00:51:54.610 --> 00:51:56.090
getting the set of people right,

00:51:56.090 --> 00:51:58.280
who are eventually gonna
turnout in the election.

00:51:58.280 --> 00:52:03.220
That has to be a huge part
of how wrong 2020 looks.

00:52:04.730 --> 00:52:07.930
And that would explain, I can't remember,

00:52:07.930 --> 00:52:10.980
Alex put up the graph about
how the Senate races are off,

00:52:10.980 --> 00:52:12.630
like the presidential races.

00:52:12.630 --> 00:52:16.050
If we've got the composition
of the electorate in 2020 wrong

00:52:16.910 --> 00:52:18.930
because more Republicans turned out

00:52:18.930 --> 00:52:21.410
than anybody thought ever
would, or something like this,

00:52:21.410 --> 00:52:23.820
then the polls are gonna
be off in both those case.

00:52:23.820 --> 00:52:27.509
So that's a leading culprit.

00:52:27.509 --> 00:52:28.847
- Great.

00:52:28.847 --> 00:52:31.840
I slightly misspoke when I
was talking about weighting,

00:52:31.840 --> 00:52:35.270
I think the problem with the
low response rate I think is

00:52:35.270 --> 00:52:39.730
even if you get the observable
demographics correct

00:52:39.730 --> 00:52:43.740
it may be that the individuals
with those demographics

00:52:43.740 --> 00:52:46.670
are not representative of the demographic

00:52:46.670 --> 00:52:49.320
you're trying to weight everything up to.

00:52:49.320 --> 00:52:50.950
Mike or Josh, do you wanna comment

00:52:50.950 --> 00:52:53.130
on this idea there are a
lot of questions about,

00:52:53.130 --> 00:52:53.963
about the polling.

