01:05:46.950 --> 01:05:48.760
So, I always says these people have

01:05:48.760 --> 01:05:52.410
a dictionary for politics
that is just a little bit off.

01:05:52.410 --> 01:05:54.810
It doesn't translate very well for them,

01:05:54.810 --> 01:05:57.413
the way it does for people
who are more engaged.

01:06:00.030 --> 01:06:02.560
- There's a "Saturday Night Live" sketch

01:06:02.560 --> 01:06:04.160
about the independent voters

01:06:04.160 --> 01:06:07.680
and the questions they demand
that the candidates answer.

01:06:07.680 --> 01:06:10.120
And I think the questions
are, who's running,

01:06:10.120 --> 01:06:12.908
is the first of the questions.

01:06:12.908 --> 01:06:16.533
Okay, so let's see.

01:06:18.919 --> 01:06:21.630
Okay, this is, just I'm gonna start

01:06:21.630 --> 01:06:23.870
going through these relatively rapidly.

01:06:23.870 --> 01:06:25.690
How long do you think it's going to take

01:06:25.690 --> 01:06:28.520
for the country to get
rid of the hate legacy

01:06:28.520 --> 01:06:30.150
of the Trump years?

01:06:30.150 --> 01:06:31.980
Again, it's a non-partisan panel.

01:06:31.980 --> 01:06:33.380
I'm just reading the questions.

01:06:33.380 --> 01:06:37.220
But I think that you
know, that's a question.

01:06:37.220 --> 01:06:39.113
So what are the views?

01:06:41.973 --> 01:06:43.403
Do you think Trump is leaving a-

01:06:43.403 --> 01:06:45.440
- It depends on elites.

01:06:45.440 --> 01:06:49.240
- Political behavior, and
what does it depend on?

01:06:49.240 --> 01:06:50.160
That's the question.

01:06:50.160 --> 01:06:52.140
- My answer is it depends on elites.

01:06:52.140 --> 01:06:55.230
It depends on candidates and
what they choose to talk about

01:06:55.230 --> 01:06:58.983
and raise to a level of
importance for voters.

01:06:59.870 --> 01:07:04.743
- So I would be strenuously
more pessimistic.

01:07:05.630 --> 01:07:07.980
Because there may have been a moment

01:07:07.980 --> 01:07:11.100
when elites could do that,
when the McCain could say

01:07:11.940 --> 01:07:15.360
he's a Christian, but Trump
has demonstrated a path

01:07:15.360 --> 01:07:19.673
to winning Republican
primaries that notice

01:07:22.330 --> 01:07:25.853
that the majority of Republican
voters do not want that.

01:07:26.940 --> 01:07:30.413
That you win Republican
primaries by not being that.

01:07:31.380 --> 01:07:33.510
So there's like no room for Republican,

01:07:33.510 --> 01:07:35.690
that kind of Republican elite right now,

01:07:35.690 --> 01:07:39.253
because that's what we're stuck with.

01:07:40.510 --> 01:07:44.230
And that's what's gonna
be the boundary for them.

01:07:44.230 --> 01:07:46.440
They're not gonna do that.

01:07:46.440 --> 01:07:48.660
- Okay, next question.

01:07:48.660 --> 01:07:51.750
I've seen some exit polls
that believe swing voters

01:07:51.750 --> 01:07:55.440
tend to view Republicans as
more focused on the economy

01:07:55.440 --> 01:07:58.640
and Democrats more
focused on social issues.

01:07:58.640 --> 01:08:02.233
Is this something
Democrats should address?

01:08:09.683 --> 01:08:13.480
- Okay, basically my answer to anything,

01:08:13.480 --> 01:08:16.450
the question that begins
with the word exit poll

01:08:16.450 --> 01:08:19.583
is I wanna answer the question,

01:08:20.530 --> 01:08:23.683
because they're just like crazy wrong.

01:08:24.660 --> 01:08:26.940
And yet two exit polls at this time,

01:08:26.940 --> 01:08:31.940
one said that Trump won white
college women by one point

01:08:36.130 --> 01:08:37.743
and that Biden won them by 20.

01:08:38.950 --> 01:08:43.150
And exits the last time around,

01:08:43.150 --> 01:08:46.609
said seniors were 16% of the vote,

01:08:46.609 --> 01:08:48.610
and it's just like all crazy wrong.

01:08:48.610 --> 01:08:50.910
Like crazy, demonstrably wrong.

01:08:50.910 --> 01:08:53.750
- Okay, so friendly
amendment to the question.

01:08:53.750 --> 01:08:54.653
I think it is,

01:08:57.967 --> 01:09:00.730
some people think that swing voters,

01:09:00.730 --> 01:09:04.770
or some people are concerned
that as a political matter

01:09:05.710 --> 01:09:10.710
Republicans are being given the brand

01:09:10.820 --> 01:09:13.350
of being concerned about the economy,

01:09:13.350 --> 01:09:16.000
whereas Democrats are
being given the brand

01:09:16.000 --> 01:09:18.760
as being concerned about social issues.

01:09:18.760 --> 01:09:21.150
Is this something the
Democrats should address,

01:09:21.150 --> 01:09:24.600
or what do you think of that framing

01:09:24.600 --> 01:09:25.570
and is this something?

01:09:25.570 --> 01:09:27.510
Again I'm trying to frame the question.

01:09:27.510 --> 01:09:29.570
- I think that these kinds of shortcuts

01:09:31.800 --> 01:09:33.620
they happen all the time.

01:09:33.620 --> 01:09:35.850
And it's natural for
people to want to do them.

01:09:35.850 --> 01:09:38.250
This is the party of this and
this is the party of that.

01:09:38.250 --> 01:09:40.890
But if the person who asked the question

01:09:40.890 --> 01:09:42.377
is really concerned about the economy,

01:09:42.377 --> 01:09:45.480
all I would say is like,
I just showed you a graph

01:09:45.480 --> 01:09:48.200
of the actual output of
the nation's economy.

01:09:48.200 --> 01:09:49.160
People get it.

01:09:49.160 --> 01:09:50.500
Like when the economy is growing,

01:09:50.500 --> 01:09:51.960
they reelect the incumbent.

01:09:51.960 --> 01:09:53.840
So if that's what you're worried about,

01:09:53.840 --> 01:09:55.810
that Republicans are always gonna win

01:09:55.810 --> 01:09:57.810
because people always want a good economy,

01:09:57.810 --> 01:09:59.260
that is not what's happening.

01:10:00.550 --> 01:10:01.383
- Okay.

01:10:02.750 --> 01:10:05.050
Do the panelists agree
with Westwood, et al

01:10:06.819 --> 01:10:11.819
that the forecasts along with,
that the polling forecasts

01:10:13.340 --> 01:10:16.590
disproportionately depress
Democratic turnout,

01:10:16.590 --> 01:10:19.583
eg. FiveThirtyEight
creating its own weather?

01:10:23.468 --> 01:10:24.360
- No.
- Do we think polling

01:10:24.360 --> 01:10:27.610
had any material effect on voter behavior?

01:10:27.610 --> 01:10:29.393
- No, not in terms of turnout.

01:10:31.580 --> 01:10:35.130
I could believe in '16 some
people voted third party

01:10:35.130 --> 01:10:38.280
because they didn't like
Clinton or Trump, or either way.

01:10:38.280 --> 01:10:40.130
Again, they thought they knew the outcome.

01:10:40.130 --> 01:10:42.820
But actually the difference between

01:10:44.040 --> 01:10:45.730
voting for or against someone

01:10:45.730 --> 01:10:47.980
is like an opinion inside your head.

01:10:47.980 --> 01:10:49.800
Going out to vote's a behavior

01:10:49.800 --> 01:10:53.023
and you're doing it for
lotsa other reasons.

01:10:54.110 --> 01:10:56.083
- Other thoughts? Okay.

01:10:57.840 --> 01:10:58.740
What caused the-

01:10:58.740 --> 01:11:01.833
- The one with Solomon
Messing, where they randomized.

01:11:03.690 --> 01:11:07.840
They randomized whether
election is being described

01:11:07.840 --> 01:11:11.500
as a win probability or
like the vote margin.

01:11:11.500 --> 01:11:14.573
And people get these two
things very much confused.

01:11:15.540 --> 01:11:18.460
So they have a survey
outcome that suggests

01:11:18.460 --> 01:11:20.910
that people are less
worried about the election

01:11:20.910 --> 01:11:23.200
when they see it as a win probability.

01:11:23.200 --> 01:11:26.683
I found that survey experiment
part of it quite compelling.

01:11:27.570 --> 01:11:29.800
- Actually, you asked the
question better than I did.

01:11:29.800 --> 01:11:32.917
I didn't understand that
part of the question.

01:11:32.917 --> 01:11:34.853
Thank you, thank you very much, Alex.

01:11:36.307 --> 01:11:39.060
Okay, what caused the high voter turnout

01:11:39.060 --> 01:11:41.710
with both Democrats and
Republicans in this election?

01:11:43.174 --> 01:11:45.572
What do you think the key factors were?

01:11:45.572 --> 01:11:47.160
Or a key factor?

01:11:47.160 --> 01:11:49.180
- Really key factor was that

01:11:50.400 --> 01:11:52.303
the future of the world was at stake.

01:11:53.720 --> 01:11:57.900
And everybody had to listen to politics

01:11:57.900 --> 01:11:59.763
every day for four years.

01:12:00.770 --> 01:12:05.770
So, right, I mean, that's
different from the last 250 years.

01:12:09.000 --> 01:12:11.350
- I'd say the proximity
of the 2016 outcome.

01:12:11.350 --> 01:12:14.020
Just how razor, razor close it was.

01:12:14.020 --> 01:12:16.740
And then, how surprising
it was to a lot of people.

01:12:16.740 --> 01:12:18.750
And then, the ease with
which a lotta people

01:12:18.750 --> 01:12:21.890
were able to vote this time
because of vote by mail.

01:12:21.890 --> 01:12:25.330
And the constant, constant,
constant commentary

01:12:25.330 --> 01:12:27.810
about filling out your ballot.

01:12:27.810 --> 01:12:31.180
I mean it was just impossible
not to be hit with.

01:12:31.180 --> 01:12:33.810
All those things probably contributed.

01:12:33.810 --> 01:12:37.690
- If polls under sample Trump supporters

01:12:37.690 --> 01:12:42.490
is an inference from that
that Trump's approval rating

01:12:42.490 --> 01:12:45.203
has been underestimated all along as well?

01:12:46.910 --> 01:12:47.743
- Sure.

01:12:50.010 --> 01:12:52.330
- I don't know that it's clear
that we're under sampling

01:12:52.330 --> 01:12:53.530
Trump voters by the way.

01:12:55.350 --> 01:12:57.250
But yes, the point is well taken that-

01:12:58.640 --> 01:12:59.840
- Like half an hour ago.

01:13:00.790 --> 01:13:02.383
We only have one minute left.

01:13:03.453 --> 01:13:04.286
- [Lynn] Oh sorry.

01:13:04.286 --> 01:13:05.460
- No I'm just kidding.

01:13:05.460 --> 01:13:06.595
Mike, is there anything you'd like

01:13:06.595 --> 01:13:07.688
to say in response to that?

01:13:07.688 --> 01:13:10.490
- The one thing that never
comes up in these conversations

01:13:10.490 --> 01:13:14.290
that's really important
is that the polling

01:13:14.290 --> 01:13:18.470
is only trying to get what,

01:13:18.470 --> 01:13:21.060
like some sense of America thinks.

01:13:21.060 --> 01:13:23.010
But that's not always
identical to the people

01:13:23.010 --> 01:13:24.543
who show up for the election.

01:13:25.700 --> 01:13:28.650
And they're not really the same thing.

01:13:28.650 --> 01:13:33.633
So that probably if you
could do a census of America,

01:13:35.100 --> 01:13:38.020
Trump's approval would be really horrible.

01:13:38.020 --> 01:13:40.713
And a census of America and the election,

01:13:40.713 --> 01:13:42.763
there's a lotta room in between.

01:13:43.700 --> 01:13:45.000
- Great, okay.

01:13:45.000 --> 01:13:48.233
We're coming very close to
the end of our time together.

01:13:49.070 --> 01:13:52.400
Each of you if you could,
do you have one more thought

01:13:52.400 --> 01:13:56.683
you'd like to share with
us before we depart?

01:14:01.910 --> 01:14:02.743
- Analysis coming out.

01:14:02.743 --> 01:14:05.810
There's like more data
collected, more results come in.

01:14:05.810 --> 01:14:08.020
And I think, stay tuned
to really learn more

01:14:08.020 --> 01:14:09.900
about the 2020 election process.

01:14:09.900 --> 01:14:11.430
Everything we're saying now,
everything you're hearing

01:14:11.430 --> 01:14:13.400
in the media, it's preliminary,

01:14:13.400 --> 01:14:15.470
and you really need to wait and be patient

01:14:15.470 --> 01:14:17.053
for all the results to come in.

01:14:19.540 --> 01:14:22.113
- Okay, I'll-
- Go ahead.

01:14:22.113 --> 01:14:26.900
- I saw a lotta a questions
in the Q&amp;A about the Texas

01:14:26.900 --> 01:14:28.300
and the Latino vote down there.

01:14:28.300 --> 01:14:30.410
And I'll just share one
little piece of analysis

01:14:30.410 --> 01:14:31.610
I looked at yesterday.

01:14:31.610 --> 01:14:33.810
We were using our data
collection, because we have

01:14:33.810 --> 01:14:37.240
almost 500,000 interviews,
we have a lot of interviews

01:14:37.240 --> 01:14:40.630
with people from very
different national origins.

01:14:40.630 --> 01:14:43.070
So Hispanic ethnicity with
different national origins.

01:14:43.070 --> 01:14:46.270
So we were looking at
Mexican national origins

01:14:46.270 --> 01:14:47.190
to Mexican Americans,

01:14:47.190 --> 01:14:51.930
in Texas you can see that
big shift toward Trump.

01:14:51.930 --> 01:14:54.000
But then we said, well
those counties are small

01:14:54.000 --> 01:14:56.790
and they're unusual in their size.

01:14:56.790 --> 01:14:58.560
Texas has a lotta counties,

01:14:58.560 --> 01:15:01.000
those border counties are small counties,

01:15:01.000 --> 01:15:04.390
almost all Hispanic population.

01:15:04.390 --> 01:15:07.780
What about the Mexican
Americans in Louisiana

01:15:07.780 --> 01:15:10.460
and Mississippi and other
conservative states,

01:15:10.460 --> 01:15:12.180
who don't make up their own county,

01:15:12.180 --> 01:15:14.770
so we're not seeing the county shift.

01:15:14.770 --> 01:15:17.249
And so we just looked and they were also

01:15:17.249 --> 01:15:20.910
heavily Trump, 59% Trump.

01:15:20.910 --> 01:15:24.120
And so, one thing to remember
when you think about counties

01:15:24.120 --> 01:15:26.970
is those are sort of arbitrary
drawn geographic units.

01:15:26.970 --> 01:15:28.910
They're not the same in every state.

01:15:28.910 --> 01:15:33.440
And so that might not be about
border counties in Texas,

01:15:33.440 --> 01:15:35.210
it might be about Mexican Americans

01:15:35.210 --> 01:15:37.280
in conservative-leaning states.

01:15:37.280 --> 01:15:38.570
And in Texas they happen to make up

01:15:38.570 --> 01:15:40.710
their own geographic unit.

01:15:40.710 --> 01:15:41.543
- Mike?

01:15:41.543 --> 01:15:45.360
- I would just say like
what Josh said, but also,

01:15:45.360 --> 01:15:48.200
not only wait until at
least February, March

01:15:48.200 --> 01:15:51.510
to have a prayer of
understanding what's going on,

01:15:51.510 --> 01:15:53.050
what the kinda questions you wanna,

01:15:53.050 --> 01:15:54.930
if someone tells you
the answer to a question

01:15:54.930 --> 01:15:58.160
you actually care about,
they're telling you

01:15:58.160 --> 01:16:00.790
what they thought before the election

01:16:00.790 --> 01:16:03.730
and found some data point that
someone threw out somewhere

01:16:03.730 --> 01:16:05.063
to validate it.

01:16:05.942 --> 01:16:08.710
And that's all and you
just gotta roll with it.

01:16:08.710 --> 01:16:10.580
- Great, and one last question,

01:16:10.580 --> 01:16:14.383
I'd just like to ask this of folks.

01:16:16.210 --> 01:16:20.540
Is there an article, a website, a book,

01:16:20.540 --> 01:16:23.110
something that you have read recently,

01:16:23.110 --> 01:16:26.120
or some source of
information that you go to,

01:16:26.120 --> 01:16:28.520
to learn something about politics

01:16:29.460 --> 01:16:31.600
that you would share with the audience?

01:16:31.600 --> 01:16:35.370
'Cause anyone who's made
it all the way through

01:16:35.370 --> 01:16:38.684
to the end of an hour and 15 minute panel,

01:16:38.684 --> 01:16:41.010
they might wanna learn some more.

01:16:41.010 --> 01:16:44.637
So what would you recommend?

01:16:44.637 --> 01:16:46.710
- I just got Edward Tufte's new book.

01:16:46.710 --> 01:16:48.750
He's a political science
professor at Yale, right?

01:16:48.750 --> 01:16:50.163
So like it counts.

01:16:50.163 --> 01:16:50.996
Really good.

01:16:50.996 --> 01:16:52.420
- It sure does, it sure does.

01:16:54.403 --> 01:16:56.813
- Alex didn't get his last observation.

01:17:00.380 --> 01:17:01.540
- [Alan] He nodded he didn't have-

01:17:01.540 --> 01:17:03.518
- Whatever said, it's
already on the screen.

01:17:03.518 --> 01:17:06.870
- He didn't take me up on it.

01:17:06.870 --> 01:17:10.440
Other sources of information
that you would recommend

01:17:10.440 --> 01:17:13.310
like for more, you know
articles that you've read

01:17:13.310 --> 01:17:16.610
that are especially
insightful or interesting?

01:17:16.610 --> 01:17:18.010
- Well, I've already pitched

01:17:18.010 --> 01:17:19.590
Lynn's book in "Message Matters"

01:17:19.590 --> 01:17:21.197
and Alan's book "Get Out the Vote."

01:17:21.197 --> 01:17:22.820
But there's a third
source that I'll point you

01:17:22.820 --> 01:17:25.330
in all seriousness, I think
Nate Cohn at The Upshot

01:17:25.330 --> 01:17:27.540
probably does some of the
best election analysis,

01:17:27.540 --> 01:17:28.820
public-facing election analysis.

01:17:28.820 --> 01:17:29.870
And his Twitter feed,

01:17:29.870 --> 01:17:31.820
I think is a really fantastic resource.

01:17:33.140 --> 01:17:34.467
- Great, thank you.

01:17:34.467 --> 01:17:38.360
- If you like the day-to-day inside scoop

01:17:38.360 --> 01:17:41.981
of what's going on, the Politico Playbook

01:17:41.981 --> 01:17:44.640
that is every morning
delivered to your inbox,

01:17:44.640 --> 01:17:47.523
but that's really for the
people who wanna deep dive.

01:17:49.750 --> 01:17:50.770
- Mike?

01:17:50.770 --> 01:17:52.480
- Yeah, I'll just repeat
what I said before.

01:17:52.480 --> 01:17:54.550
If you didn't read anything
for the last four years

01:17:54.550 --> 01:17:55.690
you'd have had a better idea

01:17:55.690 --> 01:17:58.513
of what was gonna happen on November 3rd

01:17:58.513 --> 01:18:01.813
than if you'd read what
anybody else had written.

01:18:02.960 --> 01:18:03.793
- Sure thing.

01:18:03.793 --> 01:18:05.530
Okay, well thank you all.

01:18:05.530 --> 01:18:08.450
You're amazing, amazing panelists.

01:18:08.450 --> 01:18:13.450
It was wonderful of you to
take time out of your evening

01:18:13.620 --> 01:18:18.620
to spend with us and to
give us your insights.

